-#### 3DDDA
-A ray-tracing system designed to address two basic problems in generating continuous-tone images: speed and aliasing [S4]. The scheme is based on the coherency of an auxiliary data structure imposed on the original object domain [S4]. Investigate simple spatial enumeration and a hybrid octree approach as the auxiliary data structures [S4]. Develop 3DDDA, a 3D line generator for efficient traversing of both the spatial enumeration and hybrid octree structures [S4]. Remove jags from edges by implementing a scheme for identifying edge orientation and distance from pixel center to true edge [S4]. Antialiasing time depends on the total length of the edges encountered [S4]. 3DDDA provides an order of magnitude improvement in processing speed compared to other known ray-tracing methods [S4]. Processing time is virtually independent of the number of objects involved in the scene [S4]. For large numbers of objects, the method actually becomes faster than scan-line methods [S4]. Antialiasing time is normally only a fractional addition to the time needed to produce the scene without antialiasing [S4].
+### 3DDDA
+A ray-tracing system designed to address two basic problems in generating continuous-tone images: speed and aliasing [S3]. The scheme is based on the coherency of an auxiliary data structure imposed on the original object domain [S3]. Investigate simple spatial enumeration and a hybrid octree approach as the auxiliary data structures [S3]. Develop 3DDDA, a 3D line generator for efficient traversing of both the spatial enumeration and hybrid octree structures [S3]. Remove jags from edges by implementing a scheme for identifying edge orientation and distance from pixel center to true edge [S3]. Antialiasing time depends on the total length of the edges encountered [S3]. 3DDDA provides an order of magnitude improvement in processing speed compared to other known ray-tracing methods [S3]. Processing time is virtually independent of the number of objects involved in the scene [S3]. For large numbers of objects, the method actually becomes faster than scan-line methods [S3]. Antialiasing time is normally only a fractional addition to the time needed to produce the scene without antialiasing [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using hierarchical structures for ray traversal originated in the early days of ray tracing to handle complex scenes. The 3DDDA algorithm, introduced in the 1980s, was one of the first to use spatial enumeration and hybrid octrees to accelerate ray-object intersection, providing an order of magnitude speedup over naive methods [S4]. In the context of volume rendering, the use of octrees to skip transparent voxels became prominent with the work of Wald et al. (2006), who demonstrated that octrees could significantly reduce memory bandwidth requirements for large isosurface ray tracing [S1]. The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) format, popularized by Laine and Karras (2010) and others, further optimized this by storing only occupied nodes, enabling efficient GPU-based traversal [S2, S6, S9, unofficial]. Rational parameterization methods were developed to address precision issues in floating-point traversal, ensuring robust neighbor finding in arbitrary view configurations [S1]. Presence acceleration and boundary cell techniques offer alternative pointerless or pre-computed acceleration structures for specific hardware or rendering constraints [S1][S2, S3, unofficial][S1].
+The concept of hierarchical ray traversal for volume rendering and isosurface extraction has been developed since the mid-2000s. Wald et al. introduced optimized single-ray octree algorithms for interactive isosurface ray tracing of large volumes, emphasizing the performance of single-ray techniques over coherent packet tracing for large, variant scenes [S1]. Later work extended these concepts to distance fields, introducing optimized raytracing kernels with dynamic Level-of-Detail (LOD) traversal based on projected voxel area [S2, unofficial]. Additionally, earlier foundational work on 3DDDA (3D Digital Differential Analyzer) established the use of spatial enumeration and hybrid octrees for efficient ray traversal, offering significant speedups over scan-line methods [S3].
 
